Over the past several decades, automation using robotic actuators has become
increasingly popular. Not only do robotic actuators reduce the amount of human labor involved
in a given process, but they are also often considerably larger, stronger, and more dexterous than
a human worker. Amongst the many different robotic manipulators available, cable-driven
parallel robots are some of the most effective configurations in a factory setting because of their
relatively high-end effector strength, stiffness, and extensive workspace. These systems function
by connecting at least 6 cables between the boundaries of the workspace and the robot’s end
effector, tensioning each cable such that the end effector remains stable at any given
configuration. Unlike rigid actuator parallel robots, a cable-driven robot must keep each actuator
in tension, as they are incapable of generating force in compression. In such a system, 6 cables is
the minimum number of actuators required to effectively span the workspace, though additional
cables are typically required to ensure stable operation.

This project demonstrates the stability of a Wire-Actuated Parallel Robot with both 7 and 8 Wires.
The overall goal of this project was to construct and simulate the forward kinematics of a 7-cable and 8-cable
robot, analyze the resulting change in mobility, stability, workspace, and cable tensions.
